          "question": "I have had a cough for two weeks after a cold.",
          "answer": "Based on your description, the urgency assessment is **routine**.\n\nA cough that lasts for two to three weeks after a cold is common and is not usually a sign of a bacterial infection or treatment failure. This is part of the normal course of a cold, as explained in the \"Upper respiratory infection (common cold)\" document. The \"Upper respiratory infection: antibiotic stewardship and when antibiotics are indicated\" document also states that cough duration alone within this expected window does not indicate a bacterial infection, and neither does discolored nasal discharge.\n\nNo emergency pattern in the red-flag table matched this description. However, if your symptoms are severe, worsening, or new and unexplained, you should seek medical advice.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
