          "turn_index": 1,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Would antibiotics help?",
          "answer": "Antibiotics are medications specifically designed to treat infections caused by bacteria. They are not effective against viruses, which cause illnesses like the common cold, most sore throats, acute bronchitis, and influenza.\n\nUsing antibiotics for viral infections does not shorten the illness, nor does it reliably prevent complications. In fact, it can lead to harmful side effects such as allergic reactions, disruption of normal beneficial bacteria in the body (which can sometimes lead to other infections like *Clostridioides difficile*), and contribute to the development of antibiotic-resistant bacteria.\n\nTherefore, antibiotics are generally helpful when a bacterial infection is present or strongly suspected. For example, if influenza leads to a bacterial complication, antibiotics might be used then. In some cases, such as frequent urinary tract infections, they might be considered for prevention, but this decision always weighs the benefits against the risk of resistance.\n\nYour doctor can determine if an infection is bacterial or viral and if antibiotics are appropriate.\n\n[sources: guideline-uri-antibiotic-stewardship, guideline-influenza-vaccination-and-antivirals, guideline-uti-uncomplicated-treatment, condition-influenza, condition-upper-respiratory-infection]\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
